# Goal 14: Fix `modf` NaN handling

## Function
`modf` (double-precision split into integer and fractional parts)

## Source Files
- C source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/mathd/modfd.c`
- Rust source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s6/src/mathd.rs` (around line 3422, `modfd`)

## Problem
MISMATCH — 1 test failure. NaN input returns 0 instead of NaN.

```
C:    modf nan = nan
Rust: modf nan = 0x0p+0
```

The fractional part of `modf(NaN)` should be `NaN`, but Rust returns `0.0`.

## What Needs to Change
Add or fix the NaN special-case handling at the top of `modfd`. When the input is NaN, both the integer part (via the output pointer) and the fractional part (return value) should be NaN.

## Success Criteria
- `modf(NaN)` returns `NaN` for the fractional part (bitwise match)
- All other `modf` tests continue to pass
